WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.720
Trying to comprehend
how big the Universe is

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.200
is one of those questions
that astrophysicists

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.760
grapple with all the time.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.520
Trying to explain it in a way
that's easy to understand,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.040
well that's a whole other challenge.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.240
In 1977, Charles and Ray Eames,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.040
hugely influential
American designers,

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.960
released one of the most elegant
and creative pieces

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.240
of science communication
of modern times -

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:28.960
Powers of Ten.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.280
It took the viewers on a journey from
a picnic blanket near Lake Michigan

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.240
to the edge of the known Universe,
and back again.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.520
Over 40 years later,
as a humble homage

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.000
to this groundbreaking film,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.400
we're going to take a similar journey
through time and space

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.440
and see how our understanding
has changed along the way.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.640
As in 1977,
we'll start with a picnic.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.760
Though this time, we're
on the island of Sicily in Italy,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.200
rather than Lake Michigan.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.840
We'll start with a scene
one metre wide,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.520
viewed from one metre away,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.160
and every 10 seconds, we're going
to move out to 10 times further away,

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.400
so the scene will be 10 times wider.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.560
This square is 10 metres wide,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.920
and in 10 seconds, the next square
will be 10 times that.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.320
The movement may seem linear,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.720
but we're actually accelerating
exponentially into the distance.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.080
This square is 100 metres wide,

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.360
the distance someone
can run in 10 seconds.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.880
Well, if they're running at 43km/h,
just under the speed of your cat.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.080
One kilometre, though our picnickers
are indistinguishable now,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.120
we can still clearly see the impact
of human activity on the world.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.920
Ten thousand metres, 

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.600
or 10 to the 4 metres,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.240
this is the distance a supersonic
plane travels in 10 seconds,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.680
and we're now reaching the highest
altitude flown by such a plane.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.240
10 to the 5 metres,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.240
this is the distance the
International Space Station travels

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.440
in 10 seconds.
There it goes.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.800
From here on, human activity
will be lost to sight,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.840
we're at the scale of countries.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.760
One million metres,
or 10 to the 6 metres,

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.240
we've long left Earth's atmosphere,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.080
and soon we'll see the whole planet.
What a jewel.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.400
Ten million metres.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.480
The invisible magnetosphere

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.520
shields us from the dangerous
ionising radiation of space.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.280
10 to the 8 metres.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.200
This line extends
at the speed of light.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.080
This is the time it takes for light
to reach us from the moon's orbit,

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.120
the age of moonlight.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.400
The Earth is now just a pale blue dot
in a sky full of stars.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.160
Even as we accelerate away,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.200
the stars appear stationary because
they're so much further away.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.440
So much empty space.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.600
Let's illustrate the orbits of the
planets in our solar system,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.440
otherwise this could get a bit dull.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.920
Here comes the orbit of Venus,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.280
then Mars,

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:53.520
and now Mercury.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.160
Since 2010, the NASA
Solar Dynamics Observatory

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.880
has been using an extreme
ultraviolet filter

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.960
to monitor the activity of our Sun.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.960
Finally, we reach the orbit
of the outer planets,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.400
the gas giants, but just
specks at this distance.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.440
There's the orbit of Pluto, one of
the dwarf planets of the Kuiper belt.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:16.080
10 to the 13 metres, and we're
moving out of the solar system.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.480
In 2012, the Voyager 1 spacecraft
became the first human artefact

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.640
to make this journey,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.760
followed in 2018 by its twin,
Voyager 2.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.160
Both were launched in 1977,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.120
the year the Eames were
working on Powers of Ten.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.480
We're heading into
interstellar space,

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.240
our Sun is just one
of billions of stars,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.000
and still at this distance, the
night sky looks very similar

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.240
to what we see at home.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.840
This square is 10 to the 16 metres,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.800
the distance light travels
in one year, one light year.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.960
Here's our closest neighbour,
Alpha Centauri.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.200
Three stars for the price of one,

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.120
with planets orbiting around them.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.040
I'd love to know
what's going on there.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.160
Thanks to data collected
by the Gaia spacecraft,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.440
we're building a detailed
3D map of the Milky Way.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.920
There are between 100 and 400
billion stars in our galaxy alone,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.760
and clouds of dust and gas,
like these nebulae,

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.240
where new stars are born.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.480
Images sent from the
Hubble Space telescope

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.080
have been blowing our minds
for a generation.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.360
As we move away, we begin to the see
the great flat spiral of our galaxy.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.840
A few hundred billion stars rotating
around a black hole,

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:30.520
Sagittarius A*, 4.2 million times
more massive than our Sun.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.760
we now think supermassive black holes

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.560
reside at the centre
of nearly all galaxies.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.800
These two dwarf galaxies
are the Magellanic clouds

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.120
which, together
with at least 80 others,

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.000
make up what's known as
the local group of galaxies.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.560
10 to the 22.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.280
One million light years.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.320
Soon we'll pass the supergiant
elliptical galaxy M87.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.080
And if we switch to radio waves,

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.680
we can glimpse the supermassive
black hole at its centre.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.360
Switching back to visible light, as
we traverse the Virgo Supercluster,

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.880
each tiny dot not a star,
but a galaxy.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.880
Billions of stars floating
in an ever-growing void.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.600
10 to the 24 metres.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.160
The limits of our vision in 1977.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.240
But over 40 years later,
we can show a bit more.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.200
Clusters of galaxies arranged
along filaments,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.440
like the Pisces-Cetus
Supercluster Complex.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.360
At 10 to the 26 metres, we switch
our view to microwave.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.600
We can now see the current
limit of our vision.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.320
This light forms a wall
all around us.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.160
The light and dark patches show
differences in temperature

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.560
by fractions of a degree,

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.400
revealing where matter was beginning
to clump together

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.760
to form the first galaxies
shortly after the Big Bang.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.080
This light is known as the cosmic
microwave background radiation.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.000
10 to the 27 metres.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.960
One followed by 27 zeros.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:58.520
Beyond this point, the nature of the
Universe is truly uncharted -

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.520
and debated.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.400
This light was emitted around
380,000 years after the Big Bang.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.960
Before this time,
the Universe was so hot

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.120
that it was not transparent to light.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.440
Is there simply more universe
out there, yet to be revealed?

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:14.800
Or is this region still expanding -

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.400
generating more universe,

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.000
or even other universes

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.680
with different physical
properties to our own?

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:26.240
How will our understanding of the
Universe have changed by 2077?

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.000
How many more powers of ten
are out there?

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.040
From a picnic blanket on Sicily to
the very edge of our understanding,

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:38.400
I salute the Eames for the way
they told this beautiful story.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.440
The story of the Universe.

